A decoder that drives a powered lower-limb exoskeleton is fitted once and must
keep working for weeks, while electrode impedance, montage and skin contact
change the second-order statistics that every spatial filter depends on.
Re-estimating the input covariance at inference and whitening by it, without
labels, corrects this shift inside the network and removes the need for a
calibration session. Such a layer carries a failure mode its offline
counterpart cannot have. A running estimate tracks whatever is currently
streaming, and under a protocol that presents classes in long contiguous blocks
it converges to the active class and whitens away the signal it was inserted to
protect.

We present a 24{,}181-parameter decoder built around such a layer, and derive a
lower bound on its adaptation rate from the class-block timescale of the
recording protocol. On a seven-participant exoskeleton cohort recorded across
weeks it reaches $0.868 \pm 0.026$ balanced accuracy over three data-split
seeds. Eight published decoders trained in the same pipeline reach $0.823$ to
$0.871$ at the first seed, against $0.884$ for this decoder, a margin inside its
variation across seeds. The layer reduces session-to-session covariance distance
by $45.9 \pm 4.9\,\%$ and $62.5 \pm 12.2\,\%$ on two cohorts, in 15 of
15 participants, against a control with no systematic reduction. On a treadmill
cohort whose class blocks last 309 windows, the decoder transfers at
\pending{$0.792$} without the layer, and enabling the layer costs
\pending{$0.211$}. Slowing adaptation past the block length recovers
\pending{$0.090$ to $0.098$} on every arm that uses alignment and leaves the arms
without it within their run-to-run noise, while on the exoskeleton cohort the
same change costs $0.050$ over three seeds.

The lower bound then predicted a third cohort in advance. With class blocks of
five windows, both predictions committed before any model was trained held:
enabling alignment changed accuracy by $+0.002$, and slowing it by $-0.006$,
where the same slowing had recovered \pending{$0.090$} on the treadmill cohort.
On that cohort the decoder reaches $0.796$ over three seeds, against $0.783$ for the strongest published decoder, with expected calibration error $0.061$ against $0.077$ to $0.083$. The upper bound, set by the
drift timescale, could not be measured on these cohorts. Retraining after ICA
artefact removal lowers accuracy by $0.051$ and leaves it far above chance, and
the losses do not follow the amount of muscle activity removed. A trained
selective head adds no accuracy over three seeds and is reported as a negative
result. The lower bound is a property of the experimental design, so it can be
checked before a model is trained.
